participation. So 10 years

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:51.940
later, I found myself really
puzzled by that fact. Why did I

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:55.990
continue receiving emails after
revoking my consent? Where was

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:59.200
my personal information in this
process as a high school or

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:05.890
minor? And how is it being used?
So after all of this time, a lot

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:09.430
more school and much, much more
reading. I think I finally have

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:13.660
the right words to describe what
this was. So in my thesis, I

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:16.360
argue that programs like the
College Board student search

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.360
service facilitate something
called surveillance capitalism,

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:22.150
which is a phrase coined by a
Harvard researcher and Professor

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:24.970
Shoshana Zuboff. I'll explain a
little bit more about

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:28.240
surveillance capitalism a couple
minutes. But at the most basic

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:31.510
level surveillance capitalism is
the idea that your personal

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:35.530
information traits and behavior
can be rendered into data

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.560
feeding into predictive products
that help large companies

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:42.310
provide things like targeted
advertising, and then ultimately

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:45.310
allows those companies to make
predictions about your future

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:49.390
behavior and even shape your
future behavior. So I argue in

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:52.240
my thesis, that while we need
more transparency around these

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.840
practices, our ultimate need is
greater regulation of companies

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:59.470
and organizations. So I see my
contribution to academic

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:02.890
discourse around surveillance
capitalism. And more broadly,

00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:06.760
what I'm referring to here is
critical data studies. I see

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:09.640
this as a key example of
surveillance capitalism that I

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.610
hope to convince you, in the
course of this talk, at least,

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:16.180
is entirely unethical, and the
potential implications for such

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:21.520
practice practices. So very
briefly, just a quick overview

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:24.790
of kind of where I'll take you
in the next half an hour. So I'm

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:27.760
going to take a moment to just
situate this thesis within the

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:30.550
sort of broader academic
discourse. And then I'll

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:34.300
introduce you to the privacy
framework that I build, through

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.960
my thesis to rely upon to
analyze the actions of the

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:41.860
College Board, very briefly
touch on methodology, and then

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:45.730
really dive into two key case
studies involving the student

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:49.150
search service, and then talk
about how those kind of

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:51.460
translate into predatory
marketing and the ethical

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:55.570
implications for data collection
and analysis in this process.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:59.680
Finally, I'll talk about sort of
where do we go from here? What

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:00.730
are the takeaways?

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:08.820
So I see this thesis and this is
this is sort of a question I go

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:13.140
back. Back to pretty often
within Media Studies, at least I

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:15.990
see this thesis sitting at the
intersection of what I'm calling

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:19.260
education, critical data
studies, and law and public

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:22.800
policy. So I draw from each
field in order to understand the

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:25.410
implications for programs like
the College Board, students

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:28.140
search service, in the broader
discourse around data

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:31.440
collection, algorithmic decision
making, and predatory marketing.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.100
So these are some examples of
who I've been reading for the

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:38.850
last couple of years. And I want
to bring these up as individuals

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:42.750
thinking critically about data
studies in various spaces. So on

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:46.140
the right, you see Justin's
book, Failure to Disrupt, within

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:49.290
education. We have people
thinking about this within

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:53.970
financial institutions on social
media, and in many cases, the

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:57.300
implications for what all of
these individuals are studying

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:00.990
tend to impact people of color
and people impacted by poverty

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:04.380
most directly. So I'll take a
second you see on the left

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:09.030
surveillance capitalism to
introduce you to a little bit

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:14.100
more about that. So because my
thesis uses a case study around

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:17.010
the College Board to demonstrate
what I'm calling an active

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:20.430
surveillance capitalism
impacting k 12. Students, I want

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:23.040
to take a moment to outline a
critical transition that

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:26.520
Shoshana Zuboff outlines in her
book on surveillance capitalism.

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:30.300
And so I want to explain what
she calls the behavioral value

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.880
reinvestment cycle, which is the
beginnings of surveillance

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:38.160
capitalism. So in this cycle,
this is a figure from her book

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:41.760
on the left, when information is
collected from individuals,

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:45.450
especially in an online setting,
it's rendered or turned into

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:50.100
data analyzed, and then use to
improve upon that surface for

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:53.520
that service for an individual.
So there's a certain amount of

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:56.310
data that's being rendered that
doesn't get used, and this is at

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:59.760
the top of the cycle. And
Shoshana Zuboff considers that

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:02.790
to be data exhaust or surplus
information, which is a

00:11:02.790 --> 00:11:09.030
byproduct of this cycle. At some
point, companies realize that

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:12.270
that very small amount of data
exhaust, what we're now

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.840
characterizing his behavioral
surplus was actually enormously

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:19.830
profitable. So in the second
figure on the right, we see this

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:23.490
original cycle, the behavioral
value, reinvestment cycle,

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:27.660
actually on the bottom of this
much larger image, so it used to

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:32.850
be considered exhaust is now fed
into a much larger cycle. This

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:36.330
is not intended to benefit the
user at all this entirely is

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:40.050
intended to benefit a company.
So data previously rendered as

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:44.100
extra exhaust is fed into this
much larger system that can

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:48.090
predict our behavior as users.
And that can be extremely

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:51.840
profitable. So I think what's
important to recognize here is

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:54.570
while this does feed into
marketing, and advertising, as

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:58.410
we might expect, this cycle
really goes beyond that. So this

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:02.280
new marketplace here on the top
left of the figure, operates

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:06.210
around future behavior. So it's
probabilistic in nature, and

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:08.520
companies that make the best
predictions about people's

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:12.090
future behavior end up winning
in the end. So that way,

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:14.640
marketing and advertisement can
be used to shape people's

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:18.930
behavior based on predictions
and how people act. This is far

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:22.770
from the predictions of AI
taking over the world that many

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:26.100
have made. But it speaks to a
significant reduction in

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:30.330
individuals autonomy over their
actions. So what's the ultimate

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:33.840
outcome here, individuals lose
their autonomy over daily

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:37.410
decision making. And while
companies like Google have been

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:40.920
doing this for years, I argue
that we can and we should do our

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:44.010
best to minimize the impacts of
this on other aspects of

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:47.610
society, such as within our K
12, and higher education

00:12:47.610 --> 00:12:52.800
systems. So now that we have a
better idea of the core tenets

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.740
and impacts of surveillance
capitalism, I want to pivot back

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:58.680
to the College Board, and share
some of the theoretical basis

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:01.620
for my research that has helped
me understand my own

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:04.800
relationship as a high schooler
with the College Board, and the

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:07.440
wider implications for the
College Board, student and

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:11.670
university facing services. So
in order to do so I built a

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:15.060
model of privacy based on the
work of two individuals, Helen

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:19.200
nissenbaum, and Arielle Waldman.
And I use this model of privacy

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:21.630
throughout my research to
analyze different aspects of

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:23.130
behavior from the College Board.

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:30.440
So here on the left, kind of
upper left is Ari Ezra Waldman's

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:34.760
privacy as trust. And this model
is one in which we recognize

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:38.690
that all of our relationships,
personal, professional, etc, are

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:42.530
built on trust. So we build a
sense of trust with individuals

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:45.920
or organizations to share any
kind of personal information,

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:50.240
just as we do with doctors or
with financial advisors. And in

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:53.240
those situations, those
individuals are legally required

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:57.320
to keep our information private.
So this is exactly what Waldman

00:13:57.320 --> 00:14:01.730
calls privacy is trust, and it's
enforced legally nissenbaum on

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:05.510
the bottom right here, her model
of privacy as contextual

00:14:05.510 --> 00:14:10.310
integrity is one under which the
distribution of data can happen

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:13.130
under two specific norms, and
you have to meet both of those

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:16.190
norms for distribution to be
okay. And those are called

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:20.030
appropriate appropriateness and
flow. So they help us ask

00:14:20.030 --> 00:14:22.880
questions such as is the data
being collected in a certain

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:26.720
situation appropriate, and then
is the transfer of that data

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:30.440
from one party to another
appropriately. So just as an

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:34.160
example, it might be perfectly
appropriate for a grocery store

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:37.280
to collect information on the
types of food people buy in

00:14:37.280 --> 00:14:40.130
order to restock the proper
amounts so that they always have

00:14:40.130 --> 00:14:43.400
enough in stock, but it would be
less appropriate for that same

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:47.330
grocery store to ask patrons of
the store for a list of all of

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:50.270
the restaurants they like within
a one mile radius of their home.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:54.050
Similarly, it might be
reasonable for that grocery

00:14:54.050 --> 00:14:57.050
store to share the purpose the
purchasing habits of patrons

00:14:57.050 --> 00:15:00.230
with their supplier for
restocking purposes. But it

00:15:00.230 --> 00:15:04.970
wouldn't be appropriate flow or
distribution for that supplier

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:08.630
to then share that information
one step further with patrons

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:15.080
health insurance providers. So
here I have sort of my sketched

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:18.770
out model of privacy that I use
throughout my thesis. And I'm

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:22.010
arguing for a combined framework
of privacy in this trust and

00:15:22.010 --> 00:15:26.270
privacy is contextual integrity.
So I'll start on the left in

00:15:26.270 --> 00:15:29.900
terms of assumptions. So when we
analyze this instance of

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:33.380
surveillance capitalism within
the college process, we have to

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:37.820
assume in some sense that data
sharing will continue. So this

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:42.530
draws directly from weldments
work. Second, we have to assume

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:44.960
that students and their parents,
and even their teachers and

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:48.380
administrators are not always
aware of the data sharing that

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:52.790
happens, and how these data are
shared. And this is exactly what

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:57.320
happened to me as a student when
I was in high school. So next

00:15:57.350 --> 00:16:00.980
are sorts of requirements for
data sharing of some sort, are

00:16:00.980 --> 00:16:04.430
as follows exactly the norms of
appropriateness and flow as

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:08.510
defined by Nissenbaum. So we ask
again, are the data collected

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:11.210
appropriate to a specific
context? And then when

00:16:11.210 --> 00:16:14.480
considering distribution or
flow? Is that distribution

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:17.390
appropriate? And how can
relationships of trust enable

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:21.620
and constrain distribution? So
finally, and this has been sort

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:24.260
of my biggest question
throughout all of this? How do

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:29.060
we actually do this? So I say
through two primary methods, and

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:32.600
the first is transparency, which
helps minimize any difference of

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:35.780
information between a data
provider and a data collector.

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:39.470
And the second and arguably most
important method is through

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:42.680
legal enforcement. And this
echoes Waldman's work as well.

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:46.220
So we need to build in legal
mechanisms that actually enforce

00:16:46.220 --> 00:16:49.610
data privacy legislation, in
order to minimize potential harm

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:56.330
to students. So I want to
briefly highlight the different

00:16:56.330 --> 00:16:58.730
methodologies I use in this
research just to show you a bit

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:03.020
of the varied approach that I
take. So I rely upon historical

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:06.650
research, in order to look at
the history of standardized

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:09.530
testing normalization of data
collection within our school

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:13.700
systems, policy analysis to
review all kinds of federal and

00:17:13.700 --> 00:17:17.990
state laws surrounding student
data privacy, and then I dive

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:22.040
into primary research in order
to analyze documents that are

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:24.470
associated with the student
search service in the College

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:27.860
Board. And that even includes
some of my own documentation

00:17:27.860 --> 00:17:31.520
from 10 years ago, and then
interviews to sort of situate

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:35.360
this work contextually within
the voices of individuals

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:38.480
connected to the college
admissions process. So college

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:43.340
enrollment consultants, college
counselors, admissions officers,

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:49.490
etc. So now that you have an
understanding of what I'm using

00:17:49.490 --> 00:17:51.830
to analyze the actions of the
College Board, I'll bring you

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:54.290
back to my example of the
College Board student search

00:17:54.290 --> 00:17:57.860
service to specifically look at
two examples of how the College

00:17:57.860 --> 00:18:02.150
Board violates that framework of
privacy that I just built. So

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:04.940
one example relates the
inclusion of transparency in the

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:08.420
privacy framework. And the
second use is my own experience

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:10.490
participating in the student
search service as a high

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:13.430
schooler to illustrate a
violation of the norm of

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:14.210
distribution.

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:21.140
So as I mentioned before, the
student search service is the

00:18:21.140 --> 00:18:24.050
college board's flagship program
to provide students with free

00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:27.260
personalized college
recommendations. When a student

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:31.520
participates in a college board
exam like the SAT or PSAT, a

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:34.340
student can opt into this
service and provide all kinds of

00:18:34.340 --> 00:18:37.340
information about themselves to
the College Board, such as their

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:41.390
address, their ethnicity,
gender, financial aid, need and

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:45.230
educational aspirations and
interests. The College Board

00:18:45.230 --> 00:18:48.590
then takes this information,
plugs it into its proprietary

00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:51.350
recommender systems, and then
provide students with

00:18:51.350 --> 00:18:53.030
recommendations for college.

00:18:53.510 --> 00:18:54.380
However,

00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:58.700
to kind of pivot towards the
college facing service. The

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:02.150
College Board also collects this
information, and I'll quote here

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:05.660
to create the largest most
effective admissions search

00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:11.090
database in the United States.
So that database it then markets

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:14.030
to universities and other
loosely educational

00:19:14.030 --> 00:19:18.020
organizations through the
College Board search. And these

00:19:18.020 --> 00:19:21.740
organizations and universities
have the option to lease student

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:25.430
names from the College Board in
a variety of ways. But at the

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:29.720
most basic level, it costs
colleges about 50 cents per name

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:33.800
to gain student information, as
written here on the bottom with

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:37.490
the college facing example. And
these are actual quotes that the

00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:41.000
College Board uses to market
these two services whether it's

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:44.390
student facing or college
facing. The College Board is

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:47.510
very explicit about marketing
the College Board search as an

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:50.510
enrollment solution to help
universities market themselves

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:53.930
to students. And what I think is
most striking about this quote

00:19:53.930 --> 00:19:57.200
on the bottom is specifically
this focus on marketing return

00:19:57.200 --> 00:20:00.320
on investment. This is not
towards the university. These,

00:20:00.410 --> 00:20:07.070
this is not intended to benefit
the students. So we know that

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:10.370
about 80% of students using the
College Board opt into the

00:20:10.370 --> 00:20:14.330
student search service at some
time. And even if we approximate

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:17.240
this based on the number of
people taking the SAT in one

00:20:17.240 --> 00:20:22.400
year, this is about 1.8 million
students. That's a big data set.

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:26.690
But research shows that the
student search service doesn't

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:29.900
really benefit students at all.
So according to an internal

00:20:29.900 --> 00:20:33.920
called Word paper cited by the
Wall Street Journal in 2019, a

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:36.440
student whose information is
sold as part of the student

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:41.630
search service is about 0.1%
likelier to apply to a

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:45.350
university from which they've
received marketing materials of

00:20:45.350 --> 00:20:50.330
some sort, and only about 0.2%
likely are to enroll in one of

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:55.100
those universities. So who
benefits from the students

00:20:55.100 --> 00:20:59.690
search service, it's purely the
College Board and universities,

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:03.290
the College Board brings in
about 10 to 11% of the

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:06.290
organization's revenue every
year from the College Board

00:21:06.290 --> 00:21:10.640
search and associated programs.
Universities gain access to

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:13.880
large quantities of data,
student information from

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:17.630
marketing and analytics,
individual students are barely

00:21:17.630 --> 00:21:21.980
impacted, at least in a positive
way. So this, I argue, is

00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:25.670
exactly what feeds into
surveillance capitalism. Large

00:21:25.670 --> 00:21:28.460
amounts of data are collected
and distributed to third

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:31.610
parties. In this case,
universities and scholarship

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:35.810
organizations rendered at scale.
These data can be used to make

00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:39.050
all kinds of predictions about
incoming students who will be

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:43.220
successful, who might drop out
who might major in what subject

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:45.020
who can pay and who can't.

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:50.600
So I want to take you through my
two small case studies to

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:52.700
illustrate some of the
challenges surrounding the

00:21:52.700 --> 00:21:55.130
College Board student search
service and College Board

00:21:55.130 --> 00:21:59.150
search. So this slide kind of
demonstrates the kinds of

00:21:59.150 --> 00:22:02.120
information shared either
towards students or for parents

00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:05.750
about the about the students
search service. So a student

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:08.870
considering signing up for the
students search service online,

00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:13.160
is totaled, the college is paid
for the service. Online.

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:16.790
However, parents are not told
that colleges paid for their

00:22:16.790 --> 00:22:19.490
children's information through
the students search service and

00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:23.210
College Board search unless they
navigate to a separate web page

00:22:23.270 --> 00:22:25.820
where they're told that
universities pay a license fee

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:29.180
to participate. This says
nothing about the explicit per

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:33.800
name basis of the service. So in
a test setting, on the other

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:36.860
hand, students are not told that
college is paid for their

00:22:36.860 --> 00:22:40.490
information. And this is not in
the proctor script. So a proctor

00:22:40.490 --> 00:22:43.880
administering the exam will
never say anything in the entire

00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:47.300
exam that says that there's a
cost associated with the service

00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:51.470
to universities, if and when
parents are provided with a

00:22:51.470 --> 00:22:55.040
consent form for their children
to participate, which is not

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:58.220
required on the federal level,
but certain states or districts

00:22:58.220 --> 00:23:02.030
might require. They're told the
same information as the FAQ

00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:05.690
online, which is that
universities pay some kind of

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:09.620
license fee to participate. So
this difference in information

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:12.770
is very similar to what
economists call an information

00:23:12.800 --> 00:23:16.400
asymmetry, and one that makes it
very difficult for individuals

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:19.460
to proceed within what we're
calling a relationship built on

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:22.520
trust between a student and the
College Board or parent and the

00:23:22.520 --> 00:23:25.940
college worth. So the College
Board explicitly holds more

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:29.330
information about these programs
than students and parents who

00:23:29.330 --> 00:23:33.020
are most responsible for
consenting to the service. So in

00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:36.980
doing so, the College Board has
created a resellers market

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:39.620
between students and
universities, one which the

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:43.520
larger customer the university
will default as the most

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:46.640
important. The information
students are provided with in

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:49.640
different situations to
participate in this market is

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:53.300
quite minimal. But the lack of
transparency between the College

00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:57.020
Board and students is missing.
What Shoshana Zuboff in her book

00:23:57.020 --> 00:24:01.070
on surveillance capitalism
refers to as the shadow texts.

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:03.920
And that's this individual
invisible understanding of

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:07.370
what's really happening under
the surface. So the College

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:10.310
Board tells students that the
organization is helping them.

00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:14.180
But the shadow text here tells a
very different story, that

00:24:14.180 --> 00:24:17.300
students are helping
universities market themselves

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:20.840
and make predictions about their
incoming classes of students.

00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:24.230
This is possible simply because
of the nature of a resellers

00:24:24.230 --> 00:24:27.380
market, where the larger
customer is more likely to

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:31.010
benefit from then the smaller
customer and in this case, the

00:24:31.010 --> 00:24:34.220
smaller customer isn't really a
customer at all, and is just the

00:24:34.220 --> 00:24:40.550
supplier the students. So my
second example is around the

00:24:40.550 --> 00:24:44.270
normal flow. And here's a brief
snippet of how the College Board

00:24:44.270 --> 00:24:48.110
allows students to opt out of
the students search service. So

00:24:48.110 --> 00:24:50.990
while the College Board claims
to lease student information,

00:24:51.320 --> 00:24:54.200
I'm arguing that the
organization explicitly violates

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:58.070
the normal flow or distribution
outlined earlier by misinforming

00:24:58.070 --> 00:25:00.200
students and parents about how
they disagree Have you

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:06.080
information. So as a reminder,
the normal flow circled here on

00:25:06.080 --> 00:25:09.140
the bottom is one of two norms
described by Helen Nissenbaum.

00:25:09.170 --> 00:25:12.950
Privacy is contextual integrity.
And this asks whether the actual

00:25:12.950 --> 00:25:16.070
distribution of data from one
party to another the flow of

00:25:16.070 --> 00:25:18.290
information, whether that's
appropriate.

00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:24.500
So back to the College Board, a
student signs up for the

00:25:24.500 --> 00:25:27.470
students search service, just as
I did as a high school and

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:31.190
likely many of my classmates
consenting to the distribution

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:35.120
of their data. The College Board
by claiming to lease Student

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:39.830
Information implies that such
data sharing is temporary. If a

00:25:39.830 --> 00:25:42.140
student were to agree to such
distribution of their

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:45.350
information, then it would be
expected that after a student

00:25:45.350 --> 00:25:50.060
revokes consent, ie opt out of
the program, that their student

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:55.100
data would be removed. However,
the College Board while claiming

00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:58.820
to lease student data is
explicitly giving universities

00:25:58.820 --> 00:26:03.170
data for a cost which is just
selling. So the College Board

00:26:03.170 --> 00:26:07.340
will not redistribute student
data after student opts out. But

00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:11.780
just as highlighted here, in
this FAQ on opting out program,

00:26:11.990 --> 00:26:15.770
universities that already have
student information can continue

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:19.340
to use that information and
reach out to students. So to

00:26:19.340 --> 00:26:24.470
provide a parallel situation,
which is especially funny given

00:26:24.470 --> 00:26:27.710
that it's snowing outside for
many of us today. Imagine you

00:26:27.710 --> 00:26:31.730
rent a car on vacation, says the
wishful thinking of the day,

00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:36.170
you've signed a lease with a
rental agency. On the last day

00:26:36.170 --> 00:26:39.770
of your lease, you're required
to return the car. If you keep

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:43.070
the car after your lease ends,
you're likely going to face

00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:45.950
pretty large penalties as you
violated the terms of your

00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:50.570
lease. So, with the College
Board, a student revoking

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:53.990
consent from the student search
service is the end of the lease.

00:26:54.260 --> 00:26:57.470
If a university maintains access
to student information and

00:26:57.470 --> 00:27:01.730
continues to use it, then by
definition, if that is within

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:04.880
the scope of allowance according
to the College Board, then the

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:08.180
College Board is very explicitly
selling rather than leasing

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:12.170
student information. And this
feeds directly into surveillance

00:27:12.170 --> 00:27:15.920
capitalism. An individual signs
up for service thinking it will

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:19.490
provide one specific thing and
unknowingly becomes a part of a

00:27:19.490 --> 00:27:22.850
completely different process. A
student can individually

00:27:22.850 --> 00:27:26.450
unsubscribe from universities
email lists one at a time, which

00:27:26.450 --> 00:27:30.800
in many cases could be in the
10s 50s hundreds of universities

00:27:30.800 --> 00:27:34.310
reaching out, but that student
has absolutely no idea how that

00:27:34.310 --> 00:27:38.120
university might continue to use
their information. And this

00:27:38.120 --> 00:27:41.090
misdirection is a classic
component of surveillance

00:27:41.090 --> 00:27:44.510
capitalism, students supply
their data for free into an

00:27:44.510 --> 00:27:50.810
economy that ends up feeding off
of their data for a price. So

00:27:50.840 --> 00:27:53.870
these types of data can then be
used in different forms of

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:56.960
marketing and analysis, not
unlike the emails that I

00:27:56.960 --> 00:28:00.350
received from universities 10
years ago. And so all of this

00:28:00.350 --> 00:28:03.680
marketing happens within a field
called enrollment management.

00:28:06.980 --> 00:28:10.040
Enrollment Management as an
industry helps exists to help

00:28:10.040 --> 00:28:13.790
colleges recruit and retain
students. So one educational

00:28:13.790 --> 00:28:17.300
consultant I spoke with shared
with me that this extends to a

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:20.360
College's image or brand and
maintaining prestige and

00:28:20.360 --> 00:28:23.810
publicity is very important for
colleges, both for nonprofit and

00:28:23.810 --> 00:28:28.280
for profit universities. So at
many universities, this extends

00:28:28.280 --> 00:28:31.940
beyond what we might think of as
students showing demonstrated

00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:34.490
interest by attending an
information session or

00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:38.270
participating in a campus visit.
This has even turned into

00:28:38.270 --> 00:28:41.180
measuring student engagement
with the university through

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:45.440
clicks, marketing campaigns and
metrics. So there are many

00:28:45.440 --> 00:28:48.680
companies that provide these
services to universities. And

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:52.100
some of them such as slate here
on the right, provide not only

00:28:52.100 --> 00:28:55.490
marketing services, but also
what was described to me by an

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:59.630
admissions officer at a large
university as CRM services or

00:28:59.630 --> 00:29:03.620
customer relationship
management. So here students or

00:29:03.620 --> 00:29:07.040
customers for universities and
universities need to catch their

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:10.820
eye. But looking more
specifically at the quote I've

00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:14.390
highlighted here on the right
above sleep. Many of these

00:29:14.390 --> 00:29:18.050
companies provide all kinds of
analytical services through data

00:29:18.050 --> 00:29:21.740
dashboards and other systems
that allow universities to make

00:29:21.740 --> 00:29:25.640
predictions about student
outcomes and success. So this

00:29:25.640 --> 00:29:28.520
form of prediction is exactly
what many scholars have warned

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:31.430
about taking personal
information in order to make

00:29:31.430 --> 00:29:35.180
predictions about which students
will be successful graduate or

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:38.630
be able to pay. Many times this
even happens at schools

00:29:38.630 --> 00:29:41.990
historically known for
discrimination. So this is what

00:29:41.990 --> 00:29:44.840
Shoshana Zuboff refers to as
rendition of data into

00:29:44.840 --> 00:29:47.870
prediction products and then
even further behavior

00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:50.840
modification. Student
Information is used for

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:53.180
predictions that allow
universities to market

00:29:53.180 --> 00:29:56.180
themselves to students and gain
customers through appealing to

00:29:56.180 --> 00:30:00.560
students. It makes us ask the
question, Do students maintain

00:30:00.560 --> 00:30:03.920
autonomy in this process and
what does this set students up

00:30:03.920 --> 00:30:04.880
for in the future.

00:30:07.670 --> 00:30:12.080
So in certain cases, the types
of marketing previously

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:16.460
mentioned can be helpful. And
they had that that type of

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:19.400
marketing has the ability to
bring in populations of students

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:23.330
otherwise marginalized from
higher education. But in many

00:30:23.330 --> 00:30:26.900
cases, this marketing can be
extremely dangerous. So the

00:30:26.900 --> 00:30:30.590
for-profit university industry
is a discriminatory and

00:30:30.590 --> 00:30:34.550
predatory industry that preys on
students. These schools often

00:30:34.550 --> 00:30:37.760
target students based on zip
codes for recruitment to figure

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:40.520
out which students might be
eligible for federal loads,

00:30:40.790 --> 00:30:43.220
interviewees. I spoke with
shared of the focus on

00:30:43.220 --> 00:30:46.820
recruiting military veterans as
well as veterans come with some

00:30:46.820 --> 00:30:50.690
kind of educational funding from
the GI Bill. So these types of

00:30:50.690 --> 00:30:54.170
universities use large amounts
of student data in order to

00:30:54.170 --> 00:30:57.620
optimize their potential
incoming class for profit. And

00:30:57.620 --> 00:31:00.890
programs, such as a student
search service, have the ability

00:31:00.890 --> 00:31:08.390
to enable their actions. So
